# These are the DEVELOPMENT values. Production is not configured from a file:
# Amplify holds both as branch environment variables, set by Terraform in
# terraform/deployment — see README.md → Deployment. Because they are
# NEXT_PUBLIC_*, they are inlined at build time in both places, so changing one
# in the Amplify console needs a redeploy, not a restart.
#
# Both values are inlined into the client bundle at build time, so restart
# `next dev` (or rebuild) after changing either one.
#
# To reach the portal from another device on the LAN, set both to the machine's
# IP rather than localhost, and open the portal on that same host. The refresh
# token is an HttpOnly cookie on the API's origin: browsing localhost while the
# API base points at an IP makes it cross-site, the cookie is not sent, and the
# session dies on the first reload. `allowedDevOrigins` in next.config.ts already
# permits the private ranges.

# Base URL of the Consignment Warehouse backend REST API (no trailing slash).
# The admin portal talks to this directly from the browser, so it must be
# reachable from the operator's machine and must allow this origin via CORS
# with credentials (the refresh token travels as an HttpOnly cookie).
#
# The portal must be served from http://localhost:3100 — that is the origin the
# backend allows for it (http://localhost:3000 belongs to the bidder app). On any
# other port every preflight is rejected with a bare 400 and no CORS headers,
# which the browser reports as an indistinguishable network failure.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8000/api/v1

# WebSocket endpoint used by the live auction monitor. Same host as the API.
# A single-use ticket is minted over REST before every connection attempt.
NEXT_PUBLIC_WS_URL=ws://localhost:8000/api/v1/ws
